# No Dense Format Strings

When a `fmt.Sprintf` (or `fmt.Printf`, `log.Printf`, etc.) call contains formatted values, conditional expressions, or chained method calls, extract each piece into a named local variable first. The format string itself should read like plain text.

```go
// ❌ BAD — formatting, conditional, and method chaining all crammed into one call
logs.Log.Infof("MA200: $%s %s", strconv.FormatInt(int64(math.Round(onchain.SMA200)), 10), func() string { if price >= onchain.SMA200 { return "↑" }; return "↓" }())

// ✅ GOOD — each concern named; format string reads cleanly
ma200Price := fmt.Sprintf("$%d", int64(math.Round(onchain.SMA200)))
ma200Signal := "↓"
if price >= onchain.SMA200 {
    ma200Signal = "↑"
}
logs.Log.Infof("MA200: %s %s", ma200Price, ma200Signal)
```

This applies to `fmt.Sprintf`, `fmt.Fprintf`, `log.Printf`, `logs.Log.Infof`, string concatenation in `append`, and anywhere else a format call would otherwise exceed one clear idea per argument.
